>
>     Hi Fabian,
>
>     A TERGM (not STERGM) without any temporal dependencies can be
>     applied to multiple separate groups. This essentially reduces to a
>     pooled ERGM. Estimation is possible using the xergm package, which
>     builds on many statnet functions. There are two estimation
>     strategies: structural zeros in an MCMC MLE framework (the mtergm
>     function can do this) and MPLE with bootstrapped confidence
>     intervals (use the btergm function to do this). The latter is faster
>     but becomes more accurate and less biased with larger networks and
>     more groups); the former is more accurate with smaller numbers of
>     groups but also prohibitively slow for more than a couple of
>     networks. Of course, you can also construct a block-diagonal matrix
>     with off-diagonal structural zeros manually and then use the ergm
>     package, which is basically what mtergm does for you.
>
>     Note, however, that the ERGM or TERGM does not solve the problem
>     with the violation of the i.i.d. assumption for you; you must
>     explicitly control for network dependencies in your groups for an
>     ERGM to yield results that are less biased than a GLM. In other
>     words, just adding density and node attributes is likely not enough.
>
>     Best,
>     Philip
