Message-ID: <DUB402-EAS29DA2607D466BADE6D9F5585800@phx.gbl>

Hi everyone,

 

I am a new statnet user, so I would like to briefly present myself before
asking my question. I completed a Phd in primatology last year. I am working
on animal societies and using some SNA techniques. I am more particularly
interested in the influence of individual attributes on individual network
metrics, interactional patterns and their consequences in the overall
network structure in term of resilience. 

I am currently working on triadic closure, growth preferential attachment
and homophily in primate societies. I was using SIENA but a reviewer asked
me to realize my model with ERGM model after this recent paper came out
http://arxiv.org/pdf/1506.06696.pdf. So I built this model:

 

AK.fit<-stergm(AK,

formation=~edges+degreepopularity+nodefactor("Sex")+nodefactor("Matrilines")
+nodecov("Sex")+nodecov("Matrilines"),

dissolution=~ edges +
degreepopularity+nodefactor("Sex")+nodefactor("Matrilines")+nodecov("Sex")+n
odecov("Matrilines"),

estimate="CMLE",

TIMES=1:8

)

 

I used nodefactor("Sex") and nodefactor("Matrilines") control differences in
sample size (according to this archive:
http://mailman13.u.washington.edu/mailman/htdig/statnet_help/2015/001943.htm
l).

 

For the formation model I have the following error messages:

1: In stergm.CMLE(nw, formation, dissolution, constraints, times,
offset.coef.form,  :

  Time points not specified for a list. Modeling transition between
successive networks jointly. This behavior may change in the future.

2: In sqrt(e$val/max(e$val)) : NaNs produced

 

And for the dissolution model I have the following error messages:

MCMLE estimation stuck. There may be excessive correlation between model
terms, suggesting a poor model for the observed data. If target.stats are
specified, try increasing SAN parameters.

 

Yet in Summary there is nothing particular. I would like to know what do
these error messages represent and if I have to take them into account?

 

I have two other questions:

In SIENA model there is the possibility to add for example sex rate effect
to analyses if sex has an influence on the rate of formation or dissolution
of edges. I would like to know if there are such terms in statnet?

Finally I would like to know if there is a goodness of fit test for TERGM
model (I found no example in the tutorial)? Should I use the same goodness
of fit test as for ERGM models?

 

Best regards

 

SOSA Sebastian

 

 



 

