> Alas, it sounds like you have discovered the wonders of model degeneracy -- that is,
> you've specified a model where even the true maximum likelihood estimates for the
> model would be so unlikely to produce the observed network that the estimation
> algorithm is unable to converge. This is a challenge with network models, where the
> intuition we bring from simple linear models is often confounded by the complex
> dependence in our data and among our model terms. And unlike in linear world, where
> starting with a simple model and building complexity is generally guaranteed to work,
> here a simple model can often be the source of the problem, because it doesn't
> capture the real-world processes that offset one another to prevent our social worlds
> from going off in one direction or another.
> 
> You might find it useful to go through the ERGM tutorial if you haven't before, and
> see the advice we give for diagnosing degeneracy:
> 
> https://statnet.org/trac/wiki/Sunbelt2016#WorkshopMaterials
> 
> You could also read up a lot more in depth on the statistical underpinnings of
> degeneracy if you wish, using resources pointed to on the statnet wiki.? I suspect
> your more immediate concern is finding a model that fits - and this group has a lot
> of built-up knowledge that they may offer.? I'd start by suggesting the use of degree
> terms instead of star terms; there is a direct mapping from one to the other, but the
> degree terms are generally far more stable; they're also easier for most readers to
> interpret If you wish to stay with stars, you might consider reading up about
> alternating k-stars.
> 
> Note that as you progress you might experience similar issues with your triangle term
> - if so, I'd suggest reading up about GWESP.
