>      >
>      > First: SAOMs are not a special case of a TERGM. Both are
>     stochastic models of networks but the SAOM only behaves like an ERGM
>     in is long-run equilibrium if a very, very unnatural and specific
>     setting for the rate function is used. In virtually all real world
>     applications, it does not. Also, unlike the ERGM, the SAOM is not
>     typically used to analyse networks in equilibrium. The TERGM,
>     however, is special case of an ERGM - an ERGM conditioned on another
>     network.
>      >
>      > Second: Some (Most?) of the critique IN THE ARXIV PAPER against
>     SAOMs is equally valid against (T)ERGMs:
>      > a) It is absolutely true that SAOMs have problems capturing joint
>     actions, as common in politics (i.e. formation of the European
>     Union), but the same type of emergence is, as far as I know, hard to
>     model in (T)ERGMs. It would require a clique effect, which I have
>     not encountered and I think does not exists. SAOMs probably should
>     not be used for joint action networks, but this does not apply for
>     your data. (I assume that the primates you study behave somewhat
>     like small human social networks.)
>      > b) Of course it is unnatural to assume that every single actor
>     has the exact same objective function. But the same is true for a
>     (T)ERGM. In a SAOM we assume that our parameters model the average
>     decision of the average person (given its network position and
>     covariates), in an ERGM we assume our parameters capture the
>     formation of an average tie (given its network position and
>     covariates). If we do not specify a specific subgroup (i.e. by
>     interacting a parameter with a co-variate like gender), both TERGM
>     and SAOM assume that processes are similar for all members of the
>     network (given their position in it). The (T)ERGM in fact makes
>     stronger homogeneity assumptions because not only actors but even
>     all tie variables follow the same probabilistic rules!
>      > c) Another critique is that SAOMs assume that everyone is aware
>     of the whole network state, when they make a decision. As before it
>     is clear that this assumption is unnatural, especially in larger
>     networks. But in a (T)ERGM the probability of a tie is also
>     dependent on the existence of other ties in the network. In terms of
>     information needed, the TERGM and the SAOM are no different - only
>     most TERGM papers are silent about this complete information
>     assumption, while SAOM papers are more often open about it.
>      >
>      > Third: The simulation part of the paper in section 5 uses quite
>     "weird" parameters to compare the model families. Choosing random
>     draws from a uniform distribution of -3 to +3 with steps of .001
>     does not lead to meaningful parameters for neither a TERGM nor SAOM.
>     The resulting networks will be mostly degenerate or at least will
>     not look like your average social network. A simulation should be
>     based on target statistics of a network.
>      >
>      > What I wanted to say is, that the paper has flaws. I am not a
