Hi, Christian -
That is correct: these statistics are from the sample used to _estimate_ the final model, but are not from the final model itself.  As such, they are good for general MCMC diagnostics (e.g., is the chain mixing), but they will normally understate the degree of convergence of the parameters to the MLE.  (In particular, the mean stats for the sample will often show greater deviation from the target stats than will the expected statistics under the final estimates.)

My sense is that the current consensus among the statnet team is that assessment of target stat convergence should be performed as part of model adequacy checking using gof(), which usually employs a higher-quality MCMC sample than is needed for parameter estimation and which can allow assessment of other stats at the same time.  If gof() shows poor convergence to the in-model statistics, then refinement of the initial ergm() solution is obviously in order; in my experience, however, the current code usually does a pretty good job.  Making calculation of in-model statistics part of the default behavior for gof() (it has always been available, but optional) is in the works, but hasn't been rolled out yet.

Pavel or the others may want to chime in further on this issue, but that's my understanding of the current state of play.....

-Carter


