Hi Martina and Carter,

Thanks again for all of your suggestions. We're trying out a number of
models now, and will report back.

To your question about how the data were collected, Martina, here's a bit
more detail.

I'm an anthropologist, interested primarily in understanding the social
consequences of religious action. To investigate this, I conducted two
years of ethnographic fieldwork in these two villages. Towards the end of
my time there, I developed a name-generator survey asking 12 different
questions about who residents go to for different types of social support
(ranging from Who do you discuss important matters with, to Who do you have
run errands for you, to Who do you enjoy chatting with). These are the
basis of the networks used here, though we reduce it down to include only
those who completed the survey (i.e, the residents of the two villages), so
that we are working with a full network. I trained graduate students from a
local university in how to conduct the survey; they asked the questions of
each resident in the privacy of that person's home. If you'd like more
information on the survey instrument or network data, let me know!

- Elly

